<title>Create Your Own Situation</title>
<para>Think of your own situation that could be modeled by a linear equation. Create a table of values that represents that situation. Write your problem down then switch with a partner. Make a table of values for each other’s problems. Then, check your work together. </para>
<para>Next, plot the points of each table of values in a coordinate plane. 
  Answer the questions after discussing with your partner: </para>

<!--Q#1-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Describe the scenario created by your partner. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Answers vary.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->



<!--Q#2-->
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Use Desmos to graph the table of values to represent your partner’s scenario. </item>
</list>
<para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equation that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool below. </para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
  <para>When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Answers vary.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Does your partner’s data model a linear relationship or not? Describe how you determined your answer. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The points form a line. This happens because the same number was added to each value in the top row and the same number was added to each value in the bottom row of the tables.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


